{
  "markdown_report": "At 10% progress, prioritize **matched-target stochastic prefix cropping**: a small GRU4Rec training intervention with explicit controls. No validation improvement has been established; retain the idea before exploring more complex mechanisms.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** Electronics has a 9.7% sampled wrong-rank rate versus 1.32% for CDs_and_Vinyl, motivating category-specific diagnosis without establishing validation performance. Embedding scores near 0.5 imply average cosine similarity near zero, offering little support for collapse regularization. Qualitative feedback suggests stale interests; cropping tests context dependence without establishing ownership, compatibility, or latent intent.\n\n**Insights from related work.** RSS [1] samples recency-weighted targets and changes supervision; it is not matched-target prefix removal. CL4SRec [4] supplies precedent for cropping as augmentation, limiting novelty. GRU documentation [2,5–7] establishes that zero embeddings still update recurrent state, requiring padding-safe execution. Sampled metrics [3] and validation selection bias [10] limit performance claims.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Organize experiments along context intervention, supervision control, and ranking evidence.\n\n| Direction | Intervention | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Context diagnosis | Equal-size prefix/suffix removal | Paired target-rank changes; suffix removal changes temporal gap |\n| Context learning | Stochastic prefix cropping | Matched-target full-context control |\n| Evidence quality | History-length stratification | Fixed host HR@10 and paired uncertainty |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Matched-target prefix cropping: 4/7/5.** Originality: controlled context contrast; established augmentation. Potential: inexpensive and transferable; may discard durable preferences. Difficulty: small intervention; padding, randomness, and alignment require care.\n2. **Paired context-removal probe: 3/6/2.** Originality: controlled comparison; standard perturbation. Potential: reveals sensitivity; cannot establish intent. Difficulty: few inference changes; suffix removal introduces temporal-gap confounding.\n3. **History-length-conditioned cropping: 4/6/4.** Originality: targeted augmentation; conventional conditioning. Potential: protects short histories; adds tuning. Difficulty: simple rule; calibration varies by category. Defer this and deterministic recent-window training until the primary contrast warrants expansion.\n\n**Selected experiment.** Predeclare unchanged baseline B, full-context matched-supervision control M, and cropped-context C. M/C share targets, negatives, masks, weights, and normalization. C crops with probability 0.5; uniformly remove 1…n−2 oldest inputs when n≥3, otherwise none. Retain aligned next-item targets. C−M measures context removal under matched supervision; M−B measures supervision changes.\n\nCompact nonpadding events chronologically, pack nonempty rows, run the same unidirectional GRU, then unpack and scatter outputs to original positions. Empty rows return zero features. Zeroing embeddings alone is insufficient. Use this path in training, diagnostics, and predictor; read the greatest nonpadding index, never count−1 on original left padding.\n\nKeep seeds 0/42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches/epoch, initialization, optimizer, and checkpoint rules fixed. Separate sampling, crop, and dropout streams [8]; equal seeds alone do not align stochastic execution. Deploy full histories and one checkpoint/category. Preserve routing, diagnostic interfaces, and predictor consistency. Predeclare settings, budget limits, and validation queries; record every attempt. If host per-user hits are available, bootstrap paired users within categories and equally average differences [9]. Intervals condition on models/candidates, exclude training-seed variability, and do not correct selection optimism. Monitor crop-length mismatch and popularity shortcuts; neither training margins nor training-only full-catalog ranks establish generalization.\n\nReferences: [1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643), [2](https://github.com/hidasib/GRU4Rec), [3](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/), [4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.14395), [5](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.GRU.html), [6](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.utils.rnn.pack_padded_sequence.html), [7](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.utils.rnn.pad_packed_sequence.html), [8](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.38.6.884), [9](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.bootstrap.html), [10](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Test stochastic old-prefix removal in the corrected GRU4Rec using three predefined conditions: unchanged training, full-context training with matched retained-position supervision, and cropped-context training. Compare conditions as separate experiments, and deploy exactly one independently trained model/checkpoint per category without score fusion.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests older interactions can distract from current interests, but does not establish this mechanism. Electronics has comparatively weak sampled training margins; these measurements identify difficult comparisons without proving stale-context interference or predicting validation HR@10. The three-condition design distinguishes context removal from the consequences of discarding supervised positions. At 10% research progress, this controlled intervention is preferable to adding learned context selectors or additional tuning dimensions.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the supplied corrected baseline; do not reconstruct unavailable model details. Preserve the GRU4Rec backbone, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, all four dataset routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, and 200 batches per epoch. Preserve the existing loss, optimizer, model dimensions, sampling distribution, and inference history length for the primary experiment. Set crop probability to 0.5; for n >= 3 real inputs draw k uniformly from {1,...,n-2}, otherwise k=0. This retains at least two real inputs whenever a crop occurs and includes uncropped training examples. Retain original adjacent positive and negative targets at surviving input positions; do not reconnect targets across deleted positions or pair arbitrary interior crops with the final target. Define V=(sequence!=0)&(positive!=0)&(negative!=0) and S=V intersect surviving input positions. B uses V; M and C use exactly S, identical baseline position weights, and the same baseline reduction applied to S. Skip an update consistently if its supervised set is empty. M uses the original input and C zeros the selected prefix. Report supervised-position counts because B and M/C have different supervision budgets despite equal optimizer-update counts. Do not claim equal wall-clock compute: packing shorter histories can change runtime.\n\nZero input embeddings do not prevent GRU state updates. Verify the corrected baseline's padding-safe recurrence before experimenting. A compatible implementation compacts real inputs chronologically into a right-padded tensor, packs only nonempty rows with lengths.cpu() and enforce_sorted=False, runs the same unidirectional GRU, unpacks with total_length=L, and scatters outputs back to the original input positions. Never pack left-padded tensors directly. Return zero features for empty rows, preserve B×L×H log2feats output, and use the greatest valid original position for final readout; lengths-1 applies only inside compacted outputs. Use identical semantics in training, diagnostics, and the embedded predictor writer. If the supplied baseline already skips padding correctly, retain that implementation. Any necessary correction must be shared across all conditions and documented before attributing effects to cropping.\n\nDerive separate reproducible random streams from seed 42 for initialization, user/negative sampling, cropping, and dropout. Generate the same crop plans for M and C, keyed by category, epoch, batch, and row. Consume crop randomness separately from batch and negative sampling. Matching seeds alone does not align dropout when tensor shapes or execution differ. Where the existing dropout implementation permits, generate masks in the common original B×L layout and gather them during compaction; otherwise disclose residual stochastic differences rather than claiming exact paired dropout. Do not change dropout behavior merely to obtain alignment, and preserve GRU state resets for independent histories.\n\nPredeclare B, M, and C, their checkpoint selection rule, the host-validation query cap, and a per-condition time allocation within the remaining shared 43,200 GPU-second budget before execution. Reuse only baseline measurements supplied for this experiment when permitted; never read other trials or reuse their trained checkpoints. Stop at the declared budget and report incomplete contrasts instead of silently changing training schedules. Experimental controls are separate trials and are never combined at prediction. Select the winning condition using the equal-weight four-category macro validation HR@10; retain its category checkpoints under the declared checkpoint rule rather than retrospectively mixing category winners. Every category loads one independently trained checkpoint, with full-context inference and consistent predictor code.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and ModelView.model, item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Loader batches remain (None, sequence, positive, negative), each B×L. Restore model training state after no-gradient probes. Return measured metrics with definitions and unavailable values explicitly marked. Available evidence is interaction histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps; review text/ratings and item categories/prices/stores are unavailable despite generic example schemas. Generate unseen-item full-catalog recommendations for twenty real training users, five per category, without inferring unobserved simulator traits. Diagnostic prefix/suffix removals use equal removed-event counts and fixed targets/candidates, with paired rank changes, history-length strata, and available timestamp-derived gap strata. Suffix removal changes the retained-context-to-target gap and is not a pure forgetting control. Hidden-state cosine changes indicate sensitivity rather than usefulness.\n\nUse the pinned host protocol unchanged: 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives, evaluation seed 42. If the host exposes per-user hit outcomes, use a fixed analysis RNG and 10,000 paired bootstrap replicates, resampling 200 users with replacement within each category, sharing indices between conditions, and equally averaging category differences. Report percentile 95% intervals and category gains/losses for C−M and M−B; identify these as exploratory conditional intervals rather than multiplicity-adjusted evidence. If only aggregate HR is available, report aggregates and mark paired intervals unavailable; do not reconstruct outcomes or alter the evaluator. One changed hit corresponds to 0.5 percentage points in category HR and 0.125 percentage points in macro HR. Intervals exclude training-seed variability and candidate-resampling variability, and do not remove adaptive validation-selection bias. Limit conclusions to this sampled benchmark. Training-only repeated candidate draws, full-catalog ranks, history-length/popularity strata, and margin changes are supplementary diagnostics, not alternative selection metrics. Track retained-length distributions to expose training/inference mismatch and inspect popularity strata for shortcut gains. No extra datasets, pretrained weights, hidden test access, evaluator modifications, ensembles, or independently fitted auxiliary scores.",
    "pseudocode": "Predeclare conditions B, M, C; crop probability 0.5; checkpoint rule;\nvalidation-query cap; and time allocations within the remaining shared budget.\n\nFor condition in [B, M, C]:\n  For each of the four routed categories:\n    initialize the supplied corrected GRU4Rec from the same seed-42 initialization\n    reset dedicated batch/negative, crop, and dropout random streams\n    for epoch up to 200, subject to the declared time budget:\n      for batch_index in range(200):\n        _, x, positive, negative = family_specific_sample_batch(...)\n        V = (x != 0) & (positive != 0) & (negative != 0)\n        retained = x != 0\n        for row in batch:\n          positions = chronological nonzero positions of x[row]\n          n = len(positions)\n          using the shared keyed crop stream:\n            k = UniformInteger(1, n-2) if n >= 3 and Bernoulli(0.5) else 0\n          retained[row, positions[:k]] = False\n        S = V & retained\n        model_input = x with removed prefix positions zeroed if condition == C else x\n        supervision = V if condition == B else S\n        features = padding_safe_log2feats(model_input)\n        loss = baseline_loss(features, positive, negative,\n                             mask=supervision, baseline_weights_and_reduction=True)\n        update the single model, with the declared handling of empty supervision\n      apply the same predeclared checkpoint/evaluation rule\n    save one checkpoint and its matching embedded predictor architecture\n\npadding_safe_log2feats(x):\n  valid = x != 0\n  lengths = valid.sum(dim=1)\n  allocate zero outputs [B, L, H]\n  for nonempty rows, compact valid item embeddings chronologically\n  pack compact embeddings using CPU lengths and enforce_sorted=False\n  run the same unidirectional GRU; unpack with total_length=L\n  scatter each compact output to its original valid input position\n  return outputs\n\nfinal_features(x):\n  outputs = padding_safe_log2feats(x)\n  index = greatest position where x != 0, with explicit empty-row handling\n  return outputs at index; never use lengths-1 on the original left-padded layout\n\nCompare host macro HR@10 for C−M and M−B on unchanged pinned candidates.\nIf per-user hits are exposed, compute paired within-category bootstrap intervals.\nSelect one condition by the predeclared macro criterion; deploy its one checkpoint\nper category with full-context inference. Never combine condition scores.\nPreserve diagnosis interfaces; context perturbations remain diagnostic only.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The three-condition design gives a precise distinction between removed context and altered supervision within the constrained GRU4Rec task.",
      "negative": "Recency-aware learning, sequence cropping, and matched experimental controls have substantial precedent; random prefix removal alone offers limited algorithmic novelty."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A controlled result could identify when historical context helps or interferes and justify later history-length-conditioned or learned context selection.",
      "negative": "Cropping may remove persistent preferences, create a hidden-state distribution mismatch, or improve only the sampled benchmark; qualitative feedback does not establish its proposed mechanism."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "The backbone and objective remain intact, and the supplied batch and ModelView contracts provide clear integration points.",
      "negative": "Correct packed recurrence, output scattering, aligned supervision, stochastic-stream control, budgeted comparisons, and predictor consistency make this more demanding than a sampler-only edit."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Recency-based Sampling of Sequences for Next Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643",
      "contributions": [
        "Petrov and Macdonald provide precedent for recency-aware sequence recommendation, including evaluation with GRU4Rec.",
        "RSS samples recency-weighted targets throughout a history and encodes remaining ordered interactions to predict the sampled set."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "RSS changes target selection and supervision rather than preserving strictly adjacent next-item targets under prefix removal.",
        "Its results do not establish improvements on these four pinned Amazon datasets."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "GRU4Rec implementation and documentation",
      "link": "https://github.com/hidasib/GRU4Rec",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents recurrent recommendation implementation and recurrent-state handling."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Session-oriented assumptions require care when applied to whole-user histories.",
        "Does not validate the proposed stochastic prefix-cropping experiment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that sampled recommendation metrics can disagree with full-catalog model rankings.",
        "Supports separating sampled discrimination diagnostics from full-catalog retrieval claims."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned host evaluator.",
        "Does not establish training margins or context sensitivity as predictors of validation improvement."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contrastive Learning for Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.14395",
      "contributions": [
        "CL4SRec uses sequence augmentations including cropping to construct contrastive views while retaining a next-item recommendation objective.",
        "Provides direct precedent limiting claims of novelty for cropping itself."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its contrastive framework differs from the proposed baseline-loss-only GRU4Rec intervention.",
        "Does not establish that replacing histories with suffixes improves this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch GRU documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.GRU.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Specifies GRU gate equations showing that zero input does not generally preserve the previous hidden state.",
        "Documents packed-sequence input support."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "API documentation establishes execution semantics, not recommendation effectiveness."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch pack_padded_sequence documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.utils.rnn.pack_padded_sequence.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents packing valid sequence prefixes and the CPU-length requirement for tensor lengths.",
        "Supports compacting real events before padding-safe recurrent execution."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Passing left-padded inputs directly would retain padding and truncate real events.",
        "Empty rows require separate handling, and packing does not preserve original timestep alignment automatically."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch pad_packed_sequence documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.utils.rnn.pad_packed_sequence.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents reconstructing padded outputs from packed recurrent execution.",
        "Supports unpacking before scattering features back to original input positions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Original left-padded position alignment must be restored explicitly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Common random numbers: variance reduction in comparative experiments",
      "link": "https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.38.6.884",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides methodological support for coupling random draws across experimental conditions to reduce comparison variance."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Identical seeds do not ensure aligned draws when control flow or tensor shapes differ.",
        "Coupled random draws do not establish robustness across independently trained seeds."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "SciPy bootstrap documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.bootstrap.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents paired resampling for statistics computed from aligned observations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The proposed macro analysis additionally requires stratifying resampling by category.",
        "User bootstrap intervals condition on trained models and candidates and do not correct adaptive model selection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains how optimizing finite-sample model-selection criteria can produce selection bias.",
        "Motivates predeclared configurations, checkpoint rules, query limits, and complete trial accounting."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not provide a task-specific correction for this fixed-budget benchmark.",
        "Ordinary paired bootstrap intervals do not eliminate validation-winner optimism."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
